WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.802 --> 00:00:17.904
first every day was the same for Mary.

2
00:00:18.965 --> 00:00:23.088
Every morning she awoke and found Martha kneeling upon the hearth building her fire.

3
00:00:24.249 --> 00:00:27.392
After breakfast she gazed out the window at the huge moor.

4
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:33.093
And every day she realized that if she did not go outside, she would have to stay in

5
00:00:33.253 --> 00:00:34.014
and do nothing.

6
00:00:35.055 --> 00:00:36.376
So she went outside.

7
00:00:38.017 --> 00:00:40.179
Mary ran only to make herself warm.

8
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.703
But the big breaths of fresh air filled her lungs with something that was good for her whole

9
00:00:45.783 --> 00:00:50.307
body. The air brought color into her cheeks and brightened her eyes.

10
00:00:51.608 --> 00:00:55.611
After a few days, she began to wake up hungry and eat her porridge.

11
00:00:57.933 --> 00:01:04.265
When she went outside, there was one place she went to most often: the long path outside the

12
00:01:04.305 --> 00:01:05.086
walled gardens.

13
00:01:06.327 --> 00:01:10.030
One day she noticed that the ivy grew more thickly on one part of the wall.

14
00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:14.454
She was just wondering why this was so, when she heard a chirp.

15
00:01:15.695 --> 00:01:19.438
She looked up and saw Ben Weatherstaff's robin perched on top of the wall.

16
00:01:19.458 --> 00:01:22.340
"Oh!" she cried.

17
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.561
"Is it you?"

18
00:01:24.142 --> 00:01:26.684
She spoke to him as if she expected him to answer.

19
00:01:27.825 --> 00:01:28.846
And he did answer.

20
00:01:29.406 --> 00:01:31.168
He chirped and hopped along the wall.

21
00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.622
He might as well have been saying, "Good morning!

22
00:01:35.202 --> 00:01:36.143
Isn't the sun nice?

23
00:01:36.664 --> 00:01:38.045
Let's chirp and hop together."

24
00:01:39.205 --> 00:01:40.346
Mary began to laugh.

25
00:01:40.827 --> 00:01:44.670
As the bird hopped and took little flights along the wall, she ran after him.

26
00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:46.691
"I like you!

27
00:01:46.711 --> 00:01:47.932
I like you!"

28
00:01:48.392 --> 00:01:50.214
she cried as she ran down the path.

29
00:01:51.335 --> 00:01:54.337
The robin spread his wings and flew to the top of a tree.

30
00:01:55.498 --> 00:01:57.980
This reminded Mary of the first time she saw him.

31
00:01:59.592 --> 00:02:02.733
She had been standing in the orchard, and he had been at the top of a tree.

32
00:02:03.914 --> 00:02:07.335
Suddenly she realized that she was standing outside the orchard.

33
00:02:07.355 --> 00:02:11.117
"He's in the garden without a door," Mary said.

34
00:02:11.137 --> 00:02:14.098
"Oh, I wish I could see that garden!"

35
00:02:15.699 --> 00:02:19.400
She ran up the path to the green door that led into the kitchen gardens.

36
00:02:20.621 --> 00:02:23.682
Then she ran through the walled gardens and into the orchard.

37
00:02:24.722 --> 00:02:28.724
When she stopped and looked up, there was the tree on the other side of the wall.

38
00:02:29.812 --> 00:02:31.993
The robin was just finishing his song.

39
00:02:32.013 --> 00:02:35.774
"That's the secret garden," she said.

40
00:02:35.794 --> 00:02:37.234
"I'm sure it is."

41
00:02:39.055 --> 00:02:41.495
She looked closely but could not find a door.

42
00:02:42.676 --> 00:02:47.497
Then she ran through the kitchen gardens again and out onto the path outside the wall.

43
00:02:48.677 --> 00:02:52.538
She studied the long, ivy-covered wall from end to end.

44
00:02:52.558 --> 00:02:56.379
"It's very strange," she said.

45
00:02:56.399 --> 00:02:58.820
"Ben Weatherstaff said there was no door.

46
00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:01.861
But there must have been one because Mr.

47
00:03:01.921 --> 00:03:03.242
Craven buried the key."

48
00:03:04.763 --> 00:03:06.965
Mary was quite interested in this mystery.

49
00:03:08.146 --> 00:03:13.930
She stayed outdoors nearly all day, and when she sat down to her supper that night, she felt

50
00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:15.230
hungry and sleepy.

51
00:03:16.331 --> 00:03:19.293
After supper she sat with Martha in front of the fire.

52
00:03:19.313 --> 00:03:21.014
"Why did Mr.

53
00:03:21.074 --> 00:03:22.315
Craven hate the garden?"

54
00:03:22.796 --> 00:03:23.456
asked Mary.

55
00:03:23.476 --> 00:03:27.219
"Are you still thinking about that garden?"

56
00:03:27.439 --> 00:03:28.900
Martha asked in surprise.

57
00:03:30.056 --> 00:03:31.897
"Yes, and I want to know why Mr.

58
00:03:31.937 --> 00:03:32.938
Craven hated it."

59
00:03:32.958 --> 00:03:35.919
"Oh, listen to the wind!

60
00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:39.181
You could barely stand up on the moor if you went out tonight."

61
00:03:40.222 --> 00:03:41.102
"Yes, I hear it.

62
00:03:41.543 --> 00:03:42.563
Answer my question.

63
00:03:42.923 --> 00:03:44.344
Why did he hate the garden?"

64
00:03:44.364 --> 00:03:47.526
"All right, I'll tell you.

65
00:03:47.926 --> 00:03:48.386
But Mrs.

66
00:03:48.446 --> 00:03:50.988
Medlock says we're not supposed to talk about this."

67
00:03:52.288 --> 00:03:55.310
Martha shifted on the hearth rug and began her story.

68
00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:57.251
"It was Mrs.

69
00:03:57.351 --> 00:03:59.900
Craven's garden, and she loved it.

70
00:04:01.081 --> 00:04:03.181
She used to tend the flowers with Mr.

71
00:04:03.221 --> 00:04:07.643
Craven. There was an old tree with a branch built like a seat.

72
00:04:09.203 --> 00:04:12.784
And she made roses grow over it, and she used to sit there.

73
00:04:13.824 --> 00:04:17.585
But one day the branch broke, and she fell to the ground.

74
00:04:18.846 --> 00:04:21.806
She was hurt so badly that she died the next day.

75
00:04:23.087 --> 00:04:24.067
The doctors thought Mr.

76
00:04:24.107 --> 00:04:26.908
Craven would go out of his mind with grief and die too.

77
00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:30.402
No one has gone into that garden since."

78
00:04:32.123 --> 00:04:33.564
Mary felt sorry for Mr.

79
00:04:33.584 --> 00:04:40.409
Craven. She didn't ask any more questions, but sat staring at the fire and listening to the wind.

80
00:04:42.131 --> 00:04:46.354
Suddenly she heard a strange sound almost as if a child were crying.

81
00:04:46.374 --> 00:04:48.996
"Do you hear someone crying?"

82
00:04:49.016 --> 00:04:52.598
"No." Martha looked upset.

83
00:04:52.618 --> 00:04:54.440
"It's the wind.

84
00:04:55.088 --> 00:04:58.511
Sometimes it sounds as if someone is lost on the moor and wailing."

85
00:04:59.672 --> 00:05:04.876
At that very moment, a gust of wind came rushing down the corridor and blew open their door

86
00:05:04.917 --> 00:05:05.677
with a crash.

87
00:05:06.918 --> 00:05:09.681
Now the mysterious sound was louder than ever.

88
00:05:09.701 --> 00:05:12.883
"Someone is crying!"

89
00:05:13.404 --> 00:05:14.344
Mary insisted.

90
00:05:14.364 --> 00:05:16.026
"Oh no.

91
00:05:16.106 --> 00:05:18.668
It's the wind," Martha said stubbornly.

92
00:05:18.688 --> 00:05:21.090
"Or it's the kitchen maid.

93
00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:22.912
She's had a toothache all day."

94
00:05:24.292 --> 00:05:26.415
Something in Martha's manner was strange.

95
00:05:27.617 --> 00:05:32.203
Mary kept quiet, but she did not believe that Martha was telling the truth.
